      createBlock("Why Do Restaurants Put One Ridiculously Expensive Dish on the Menu?", "h2"),
      createBlock("When you open a high-end restaurant menu, your eyes will frequently land on an outrageous item: a $180 Grand Seafood Platter or a $220 32oz Dry-Aged Tomahawk Steak with Gold Leaf. You probably scoff internally. 'Who on earth orders that?' you wonder."),
      createBlock("Here is the brilliant, manipulative secret of menu engineering: The restaurant does not actually expect many guests to order that $180 dish. Its primary purpose on the menu is not culinary; it is purely psychological. It is what behavioral economists call a 'Price Anchor'."),
      createBlock("1. The Relativity of Numbers and the Contrast Effect", "h3"),
      createBlock("Imagine walking into a store and seeing a $45 steak on a menu where most items are $20. Paying $45 feels incredibly steep. But when your eye first encounters a $180 anchor at the top of the page, your internal pricing scale resets. Suddenly, that $45 steak feels modest, reasonable, and safe by comparison. It feels like a smart financial decision."),
      createBlock("This is the Contrast Effect in action. Human beings are terrible at judging absolute numerical value. If I ask you if $45 is objectively 'a lot' for a piece of meat, you struggle to answer. But we are exceptionally good at judging comparative differences. By anchoring the top of the scale at an extreme number, the restaurant deliberately shifts everything beneath it into the 'acceptable' cognitive zone. It's the same reason car dealerships put the $120,000 sports car right at the entrance, even though you came to buy a $30,000 sedan."),
      createBlock("2. Omitting Dollar Signs ($) to Reduce the 'Pain of Paying'", "h3"),
      createBlock("Have you noticed that modern, trendy restaurants rarely use currency symbols anymore? You don't see '$38.00'. You just see a solitary '38' floating elegantly next to the dish description."),
      createBlock("This isn't just minimalist graphic design; it's heavily researched behavioral science. Cornell University researchers proved that menus that omit dollar signs cause diners to spend an average of 8.1% more. Why? Because the currency symbol physically activates a region in the brain called the insular cortex—the same area associated with processing physical pain. It triggers the 'Pain of Paying.' Removing the symbol elegantly decouples the gastronomic enjoyment from the visceral sensation of financial loss."),
      createBlock("3. The Decoy Effect (The Goldilocks Strategy)", "h3"),
      createBlock("Another classic menu trick is placing three distinct sizes of wine: a 5oz glass for $12, an 8oz glass for $16, and a bottle for $45. The 5oz glass acts as a decoy. Most people don't want to look cheap (avoiding the smallest), but they don't want to overindulge (avoiding the bottle). They predictably settle right in the middle, feeling they've made a highly rational compromise. You didn't make a choice; the menu made it for you.")
